{
  "id": "20261101-180123-d476",
  "hypothesis": "Enabling flash attention (flash_attn=1) on the CUDA backend at the pinned commit changes balanced performance on this SM 6.1 GPU. Pascal has no tensor cores, so llama.cpp uses its tile and vec FA kernels instead of the MMA path. The expected outcomes are lower VRAM (no materialised KQ matrix in the compute buffer), a decode speed within noise or slightly higher (the vec kernel fuses softmax and avoids extra KQ/KQV launches), and an uncertain prompt-processing result: the tile kernel may be slower than cuBLAS/MMQ KQ matmuls at pp512/pp2048. Quality should be unchanged within measured noise because the KV cache stays f16.",
  "research_objective": "Isolate the effect of flash attention alone, with KV cache types held at f16, on pp512, pp2048, tg128, TTFT, VRAM peak, host RSS and tokens/J. This is the prerequisite for H008: the later q8_0 KV cache test requires flash_attn=1, so FA's own cost or benefit must be known first so the KV quantisation effect can be separated from it.",
  "description": "Single-variable change from the validated baseline (baseline-2026-11-01T175223+0000): runtime.flash_attn 0 -> 1. Everything else stays the same: same build, commit 7bd18083 / b12021, no cmake flags, no patches, f16 K/V, b=2048, ub=512, ngl=99, mmap=1, no env. The quality check should report KLD vs the q8_0 reference close to the baseline 0.020544 and PPL close to 11.8775. Interpretation plan: if pp drops beyond noise while tg and VRAM improve, the record shows a real tradeoff, and FA becomes a candidate only when paired with q8_0 KV in a follow-up. If FA is neutral or positive across all metrics, the next step is cache_type_k/v=q8_0 on top of it to test H008.",
